</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare the proposed
ProgD with a series of state-of-the-art joint multi-agent motion prediction methods. First, graph neural network-based methods are taken into account. For instance, Lanegcn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and ReCoG2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo et al., 2020</a>)</cite> employ graph neural networks to encode observed scenarios, and decode the future trajectory with MLPs or RNNs. Based on the representation of observed scenarios from GNNs, DenseTNT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> improves on decoding with offline optimization-based technique; THOMAS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilles et al., 2022a</a>)</cite> recombines candidate marginal predictions to obtain consistent joint predictions with attention schemes; FFINet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces a feedback module to consider the influence
of future interactions; FJMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rowe et al., 2023</a>)</cite> further exploits graph convolutional networks to perform marginal and conditional inference but is still limited to using static graphs to handle future interactions. Furthermore, various Transformer-based methods are considered. For instance, hierarchical vector Transformers and heterogeneous graph Transformers are respectively explored in HiVT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and HDGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to handle the diverse interactions in observed scenarios. EEGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> further designs an edge-enriched graph transformer to handle the complex interactions. AutoBots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Girgis et al., 2022</a>)</cite> implicitly handles future interactions through alternative attention layers across time and agents in the decoder. Similarly, Forecast-MAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite> adopts a Transformer-based autoencoder and is trained in an unsupervised way. Built upon AutoBots, Traj-MAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> resorts to masking strategies for consistent prediction, and MS-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> exploits an evolutionary training process to enhance generalizability. Additionally, DGFNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> considers prediction difficulty among agents with a difficulty-guided decoder. MIND <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> further combines prediction with planning tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experimental Results and Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As presented in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ProgD: Progressive Multi-scale Decoding with Dynamic Graphs for Joint Multi-agent Motion Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the proposed ProgD outperforms a collection of state-of-the-art methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rowe et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> on the INTERACTION multi-agent leaderboard in terms of the Consistent-minJointMR ranking metric, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ranking the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1^{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1^{st}</annotation></semantics></math></span> from May 22, 2024 to March 14, 2025. As evaluated in terms of minJFDE and minJMR, ProgD achieves the best performance in accuracy. It reduces the minJFDE from 0.9218 to 0.8620 and decreases the minJMR from 0.1728 to 0.1538. This improvement is probably attributed to multiscale decoding, which prioritizes keyframe prediction (e.g., final positions within each time interval) before refining the complete trajectory. This strategy, while effective, may result in a slight increase in minJADE values.
Furthermore, ProgD demonstrates competitive or superior performance in terms of consistency metrics, especially reducing the ego collision rate egoCR from 0.0044 to 0.0011. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ProgD: Progressive Multi-scale Decoding with Dynamic Graphs for Joint Multi-agent Motion Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, trajectories predicted by ProgD demonstrate superior adherence to the road network structure, while also exhibiting consistency among highly interactive agents. For instance, Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ProgD: Progressive Multi-scale Decoding with Dynamic Graphs for Joint Multi-agent Motion Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (a) illustrates that the baseline model erroneously predicts the bottom vehicle to be traveling in the wrong lane, whereas ProgD accurately predicts the vehicle to be in the correct lanes, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ProgD: Progressive Multi-scale Decoding with Dynamic Graphs for Joint Multi-agent Motion Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b).</p>
</div>
